<p>These terms are the agreement between us and the business that uses Fileready ("your firm" or "you"). Fileready is a service for businesses, not consumers. By creating an account, starting a trial or subscribing, you accept these terms on behalf of your firm, and you confirm that you are allowed to do so.</p>

<h2>1. Who we are</h2>
<%- include('_who') %>

<h2>2. The service</h2>
<p>Fileready lets your firm request documents from your clients, gives each client a secure upload page, sends reminders by email (and by text, if you switch that on), and lets your team review, store and download what arrives. We keep improving Fileready and may change how it works. If a change would significantly reduce what the service does for you, we'll tell you at least 30 days before it happens.</p>

<h2>3. Your accounts</h2>
<p>Your firm's owners decide who has a login and what they can do. You are responsible for everything done under your firm's logins. Keep passwords private, remove access promptly when someone leaves, and tell us straight away if you think a login has been misused. We strongly recommend that everyone turns on two-step login.</p>

<h2>4. Your clients' data</h2>
<p>Your firm is the controller of the personal data about your clients that you put into Fileready or that they upload. That means you must:</p>
<ul>
  <li>have a lawful basis for collecting it and for contacting clients by email or text;</li>
  <li>tell your clients, in your own privacy notice, that you use Fileready to collect and store their documents;</li>
  <li>request only the documents you need, and keep clients' contact details accurate;</li>
  <li>deal with your clients' data protection requests, using the tools Fileready provides (editing, exporting and deleting).</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/dpa">data processing agreement</a> forms part of these terms. It sets out how we handle your clients' data on your behalf.</p>

<h2>5. Acceptable use</h2>
<p>You must not use Fileready to break the law, to send unwanted messages, or to collect documents for any purpose other than your firm's business. You must not try to get around its security, access another firm's data, test its security without our written permission, overload it, copy or reverse engineer it, or resell it. We may suspend access if we reasonably believe these rules are being broken, and we'll tell you why.</p>

<h2>6. Free trial, fees and payment</h2>
<p>A free trial lasts for the period shown when you sign up. After that, continuing to use Fileready needs a paid subscription. The price is the one shown on the checkout page when you subscribe, and the checkout page shows whether VAT is added. Subscriptions are billed in advance each period through Stripe, and renew automatically until cancelled.</p>
<p>If a payment fails, we'll let you know. If it is still unpaid 14 days later, we may limit your firm's account until it is paid. We'll give you at least 30 days' notice by email before any price change applies to you.</p>

<h2>7. Cancelling and ending the agreement</h2>
<p>You can cancel at any time from the billing page. Cancellation takes effect at the end of the period you've already paid for, and we don't refund partly used periods unless the law requires it.</p>
<p>We may end this agreement if you seriously break these terms and don't fix the problem within 14 days of us telling you, or straight away if the breach can't be fixed. We may also stop providing Fileready altogether by giving you at least 60 days' notice and refunding any fees paid for the period after it ends.</p>

<h2>8. Your data when the agreement ends</h2>
<p>While your account is active you can download any case as a zip file. When the agreement ends, you have 30 days to ask us for an export of your firm's data. After those 30 days we delete your firm's data, except billing records that we must keep by law.</p>

<h2>9. Availability and support</h2>
<p>We work to keep Fileready available and secure, but we can't promise it will always be uninterrupted or free of errors. We may need to take it offline briefly for maintenance, and we'll try to do that outside business hours.<% if (legal.email) { %> For help, email <a href="mailto:<%= legal.email %>"><%= legal.email %></a>; we aim to reply within one working day.<% } %></p>

<h2>10. Ownership</h2>
<p>We own Fileready, its software and its design. Your firm owns its data, including your clients' details and documents. You give us permission to handle that data only as needed to provide Fileready to you and as described in the data processing agreement. If you send us suggestions, we may use them without any obligation to you.</p>

<h2>11. Confidentiality</h2>
<p>Each of us will keep the other's non-public business information confidential and use it only for this agreement, unless the law requires it to be disclosed.</p>

<h2>12. Liability</h2>
<p>Nothing in these terms limits liability for death or personal injury caused by negligence, for fraud, or for anything else that can't be limited by law.</p>
<p>Apart from that, neither of us is liable for loss of profits, revenue, business or goodwill, or for any indirect or consequential loss. Our total liability to you under or in connection with this agreement in any 12-month period is limited to the greater of the fees you paid us in the 12 months before the claim arose and €100.</p>

<h2>13. Changes to these terms</h2>
<p>We may update these terms. If a change is significant, we'll email your firm's owners at least 30 days before it takes effect. If you don't agree with the change, you can cancel before then.</p>

<h2>14. General</h2>
<p>These terms, together with the data processing agreement and the price shown at checkout, are the whole agreement between us about Fileready. You may not transfer this agreement to someone else without our written consent. We may transfer it to a business that takes over Fileready, and we'll tell you if that happens. Notices will be sent by email: to your firm's owners, and to us at the address above. If any part of these terms is found to be unenforceable, the rest still applies. These terms are governed by Irish law, and the courts of Ireland have exclusive jurisdiction.</p>
